5. Explain the importance and major advantages of relational databases.
Importance:
Relational databases remain the backbone of most enterprise transactional systems because they enforce data integrity, support complex queries, and provide strong consistency.
Major Advantages:
Strong ACID compliance (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability)
Well-defined schema and data integrity constraints
Powerful declarative query language (SQL)
Mature ecosystem, tools, and standards
Excellent support for complex joins and transactions
Proven reliability for financial, ERP, and critical business systems
6. What is persistent data? Explain its importance in database systems.
Persistent data is data that remains available even after the program or system that created it terminates. It is stored on non-volatile storage (hard disk, SSD, etc.).
Importance:
Ensures data survives system crashes, power failures, or restarts
Enables long-term storage and retrieval of business-critical information
Forms the foundation of reliable database systems (durability aspect of ACID/BASE)
Allows historical analysis, auditing, and recovery
7. What is concurrency? Explain why concurrency is required in database systems.
Concurrency is the ability of a database system to allow multiple users or transactions to access and modify data simultaneously.
Why it is required:
Modern applications serve thousands or millions of concurrent users
Improves system throughput and resource utilization
Essential for multi-user environments (banking, e-commerce, social media)
Without concurrency control, data inconsistency, lost updates, and dirty reads would occur
8. Explain database integration and its importance in modern applications.
Database integration is the process of combining data from multiple databases or data sources into a unified view so that applications can access and use the data as if it came from a single source.
Importance in modern applications:
Organizations typically use multiple specialized databases (SQL + NoSQL)
Enables real-time data sharing across microservices
Supports analytics, reporting, and decision-making
Reduces data silos
Facilitates hybrid architectures (polyglot persistence)
9. What is Impedance Mismatch? Explain with a suitable example.
Impedance Mismatch refers to the difference between the object-oriented model used in application code and the relational table model used in RDBMS.
Example:
In an object-oriented language, a Student object may contain nested objects (Address, list of Courses). In a relational database, this must be flattened into multiple tables (Students, Addresses, Courses, Student_Courses) with foreign keys. Mapping between the two models requires ORMs (Object-Relational Mappers) and often leads to complex joins and performance issues.
NoSQL document databases reduce this mismatch because documents can closely mirror application objects.
